WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.509
A bar chart shows data in separate groups, called categories. Here, the categories are the ways pupils travel to school: walk, cycle, bus and car. The height of each bar shows how many pupils.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:30.758
Why a bar chart? The data are discrete. Discrete means separate groups, with nothing in between. Time or temperature are continuous: every value in between is possible. Continuous data go on a line graph.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:41.720
Step one. Put the categories on the x-axis, and the values on the y-axis. Label the y-axis with the quantity and its unit, like energy in kilojoules per gram.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:59.232
Step two. Choose a scale with equal steps, starting at zero. It must use more than half of the grid. These are made-up example values for four foods. The biggest value is 15, so steps of 2 up to 16 fill the grid well.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:07.056
Step three. Draw each bar to the right height with a ruler. Be accurate to half a small square.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:16.593
Step four. Make every bar the same width, with equal gaps between them. Then write the name of each category under its bar.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:34.463
Here is a real exam question. The table shows the chance of a baby being born with a chromosome aberration, for mothers of different ages. A chromosome aberration is a change to a whole chromosome. Plot a bar chart of the results.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:57.174
Here is the table. Mothers aged 15 to 19, 20 to 24 and 25 to 29 each have a probability of 0.0010. Aged 30 to 34, it is 0.0015. Aged 35 to 39, it is 0.0045. Aged 40 to 44, it is 0.0140.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:16.352
The largest value is 0.0140. The grid is 8 medium squares tall. If each medium square is 0.002, then 0.0140 needs 7 of them. That fills most of the grid, with equal steps from zero.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:35.367
Now draw six bars of equal width, with equal gaps. The first three are 0.0010. Then 0.0015, then 0.0045. The last one, for mothers aged 40 to 44, is 0.0140.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:48.157
Both axes are labelled: age of mother in years, and probability of chromosome aberration. Each bar has its age group. Explanation by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:58.108
A common mistake is drawing a line graph for categories. The age groups are separate, so the data need bars.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:07.010
Another mistake is giving the reason for a bar chart as it is easier to read. The reason is that the data are in separate categories, so they are discrete.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:24.944
Your turn. A class records how many pupils have each blood group: O 14, A 10, B 4, and AB 2. Describe how you would plot a bar chart of these results, and say why a bar chart is suitable. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:37.083
Blood groups go on the x-axis. Number of pupils goes on the y-axis. The biggest value is 14, so use steps of 2 from zero up to 16.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:49.290
Draw four bars of equal width: 14, 10, 4 and 2. Name each bar. A bar chart suits these data because blood groups are separate categories.

00:03:49.850 --> 00:04:02.471
So, every time: categories across, values up. Label the axes with units. Use an even scale from zero. Draw equal bars to the right heights.
